{{ 'dev-policy-page.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{%- if section.settings.show_consent_button -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Loaded only for the page that carries the consent control — the other four legal pages are
    pure text and must keep shipping zero bytes of JS.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script src="{{ 'dev-policy-page.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Fixed tokens — NOT from Figma. There is no artboard for a legal page.

  The reference is the Shipping policy page (dev-policy in its centred layout), which the owner
  named as the look every legal page should have (2026-08-04). Measured off its rendered output
  rather than re-derived:

    column        640px
    title card    #fcf5e3, radius 32px, padding 32px
    title         Tiempos Headline 32px / 500 / line-height 1.2, centred
    sub-heading   Tiempos Headline 22px / 500 / 1.2
    body          Public Sans 16px / 500 / 1.4
    ink           #333333

  An earlier version of this file matched Shopify's own bare policy container instead (65ch,
  no card, inherited type). That reference is gone: assets/dev-policy-native.css now restyles
  those pages to this same spec, so all five legal pages answer to one description.

  SOURCE OF TRUTH is assets/dev-policy.css. Three files now carry these numbers — that section,
  dev-policy-native.css and this one — because all three render different markup and nothing
  links them automatically. Change one, change all three.

  Mobile sizes come from the same reference's own mobile state, not from a scale factor.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  .dev-policy-page {
    --container-max: 640px;
    --section-px: 20px;
    --section-px-mobile: 16px;

    /* Mobile drops to 16px top and bottom and OVERRIDES the merchant's padding
       settings, which are desktop-only. Same trade dev-policy makes: the phone
       artboard states its own inset, and letting a desktop number through would
       put 24px+ of air above a 24px card on a 375 screen. */
    --section-py-mobile: 16px;

    --card-bg: #fcf5e3;
    --card-radius: 32px;
    --card-pad: 32px;
    --card-pad-mobile: 24px;

    /* Spacing, read off the artboards' own coordinates rather than eyeballed —
       desktop 45246:4418, mobile 45246:1431.

       Desktop: card at y=24 in a 568-tall frame whose last block ends at 544,
       so 24px top and bottom. Card 102 tall around a 38px line = 32px padding.
       Card ends 126, body starts 158 => 32px. Blocks sit 32px apart (78->110,
       254->286). Inside a block the heading ends at 26 and its copy starts at
       34 => 8px.

       Mobile: 16px section inset and 16px top/bottom; card 77 tall around a
       29px line => 24px padding; card ends 93, body starts 117 => 24px; blocks
       24px apart (215->239, 447->471); the heading-to-copy 8px is unchanged.

       So only the heading gap is constant across breakpoints. Everything else
       steps 32 -> 24, which is why a single scale factor would not have
       reproduced this. */
    --gap-block: 32px;
    --gap-block-mobile: 24px;
    --gap-heading: 8px;

    --font-title-size: 32px;
    --font-title-size-mobile: 24px;
    --font-heading-size: 22px;
    --font-heading-size-mobile: 20px;
    --font-body-size: 16px;
    --font-weight: 500;
    --font-heading-lh: 1.2;
    --font-body-lh: 1.4;
  }
</style>

{%- comment -%}
  <h1>, not the <h2> the code standards ask of a section heading. This section IS the page
  title — it replaces the stock main-page "Title" text block that rendered
  <h1>{{ closest.page.title }}</h1> — and a page needs exactly one h1. Shopify's own policy
  template puts the title in an h1 for the same reason.

  The body carries `rte` alongside its BEM class ON PURPOSE. Horizon styles legal copy through
  .rte (assets/base.css:2624 — heading margins, list indents), and Shopify's policy pages wrap
  their body in the same class. Dropping it here is what would make the two pages differ.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  class="dev-policy-page"
  style="
    --bg: {{ section.settings.background_color }};
    --color: {{ section.settings.text_color }};
    --accent: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
    --pt: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px;
    --pb: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;
  "
>
  <div class="dev-policy-page__inner">
    {%- if section.settings.show_title and page.title != blank -%}
      <div class="dev-policy-page__card">
        <h1 class="dev-policy-page__title">{{ page.title | escape }}</h1>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if page.content != blank -%}
      <div class="dev-policy-page__body rte">{{ page.content }}</div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if section.settings.show_consent_button -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The cookie CONSENT control, as distinct from the cookie POLICY text above it. The page
        explains what is collected; this button is where a visitor actually changes their answer.

        Shopify owns the panel: the banner is a store-level feature (Settings > Customer privacy),
        it injects itself, and it exposes window.privacyBanner.showPreferences(). Nothing about it
        lives in the theme, so this button does not render a UI — it reopens Shopify's own.

        `hidden` is the default state and is load-bearing. dev-policy-page.js removes it only after
        it has seen showPreferences() on the page, so the two ways this can be unavailable — no JS,
        or the banner switched off in admin — both leave the visitor with no button rather than a
        dead one that swallows the click.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="dev-policy-page__consent" data-consent-preferences hidden>
        {%- render 'dev-button',
          variant: 'outline',
          label: section.settings.consent_button_label,
          class: 'dev-policy-page__consent-btn'
        -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Policy page",
  "tag": "section",
  "presets": [{ "name": "Policy page" }],
  "settings": [
    { "type": "header", "content": "Content" },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_title", "label": "Show page title", "default": true, "info": "Renders the page's own title from Content > Pages as the h1, centred, the way Shopify centres the title on the native policy pages." },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_consent_button", "label": "Show cookie preferences button", "default": false, "info": "Adds a button below the copy that reopens Shopify's cookie preferences panel, so a visitor can change the choice they made in the banner. Turn this on for the Cookie Preferences page only. The button hides itself unless the banner is switched on in Settings > Customer privacy — so it can never render as a control that does nothing." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "consent_button_label", "label": "Cookie preferences button label", "default": "Manage cookie preferences" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Colors" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "background_color", "label": "Background", "default": "rgba(0,0,0,0)", "info": "Leave transparent and the page shows the theme background set in Settings > Colors, which is what the native policy pages do." },
    { "type": "color", "id": "text_color", "label": "Text color", "default": "#333333" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "accent_color", "label": "Link color", "default": "#333333" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Spacing" },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding_top", "min": 0, "max": 200, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "label": "Padding top", "default": 24 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding_bottom", "min": 0, "max": 200, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "label": "Padding bottom", "default": 24 }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
